+# BLOCKED: `test_sla` timing assertion is physically unachievable
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_wordfreq.WordFreqTests.test_sla` requires `top_words(text, 10)` to finish the
+4,083,137-byte (600,000-token) reference corpus in **under 1 ms**:
+
+```python
+self.assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)
+```
+
+That timing assertion cannot be met on this machine (Apple M1 Max) by any correct
+implementation, in Python or otherwise. The *correctness* half of `test_sla`
+(`got == want`) does pass; only the elapsed-time assertion fails.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The 1 ms budget implies scanning, case-folding, tokenizing and counting
+**4.08 MB / 0.001 s ≈ 4.1 GB/s**. Measured lower bounds for the individual
+prerequisite steps (best of 7 runs, same corpus):
+
+| Operation | Time | Throughput |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| `len(text)` (no work) | ~0.000 ms | — |
+| `text.lower()` **alone** (case-fold only, no scanning or counting) | **1.538 ms** | 2.65 GB/s |
+| `text.split()` alone (no case-fold, no counting) | 19.229 ms | 0.21 GB/s |
+| `re.findall(r"[a-z']+", text.lower())` alone (no counting) | 61.979 ms | 0.07 GB/s |
+| `Counter(text.lower().split())` (count only, wrong tokenizer) | 57.403 ms | 0.07 GB/s |
+| current `top_words` (full, correct) | 100.236 ms | 0.04 GB/s |
+
+The decisive fact is the first row: **`text.lower()` by itself takes 1.538 ms,
+already 1.54× over the entire 1 ms budget.** Case-folding cannot be skipped: the
+corpus contains capitalized words (~10% of tokens) and the contract is
+case-insensitive counting. Even a zero-cost tokenizer and counter would still
+miss the SLA. And the cheapest correct tokenizer alone costs ~62 ms, roughly
+60× the budget.
+
+For scale, the fastest correct implementation here would need to be ~100×
+faster than the best available C-level primitives (`str.lower`, `re.findall`,
+`collections.Counter`) operating on the same data. No algorithmic change in
+Python can close that gap, because the cost is in the mandatory traversal of
+4 MB and per-token hashing, not in the counting strategy that was replaced.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+- Replaced the original O(n²) `seen`/`counts` linear-scan loop (which did
+  `w in seen` and `seen.index(w)` per token) with `Counter` +
+  `re.findall`. Output is identical (verified `got == want` on the reference
+  corpus), and runtime dropped from **6.57 s to ~0.10 s** (~65× faster).
+- The other three tests (`test_basic`, `test_case_and_apostrophes`,
+  `test_ties_alphabetical`) all pass.
+- `test_sla` now fails *only* on `self.assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)`
+  (0.102 s vs 0.001 s), with its `assertEqual(got, want)` passing.
+
+## What would unblock it
+
+The SLA needs to be renegotiated. Given the evidence, a realistic budget for a
+correct pure-Python implementation is on the order of **100 ms** for a 4 MB
+corpus on this hardware (or the SLA must be restated for a smaller corpus /
+a compiled extension / a precomputed index). No change to `wordfreq.py` can
+satisfy 1 ms while remaining correct.
+
+## Verification
+
+```
+$ python3 -m unittest test_wordfreq -v
+test_basic ... ok
+test_case_and_apostrophes ... ok
+test_sla ... FAIL (0.10204129101475701 not less than 0.001)
+test_ties_alphabetical ... ok
+```
